<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soča</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isonzo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Soča, in Italian Isonzo, is a river about 140 kilometres long that rises in the Julian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> into the Adriatic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> on its banks in 489 on his way into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> twelve battles of the Isonzo were fought along it between Italy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1915–1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soca.jpg" alt="Soča" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florian Jesse · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soca_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Soča, known in Italian as Isonzo, is a river 138 kilometres long that flows through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> for 96 kilometres and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for 43 kilometres. Its source lies in the Trenta Valley in the Julian Alps at an elevation of 876 metres. It runs past Bovec, Kobarid, Tolmin, Kanal ob Soči, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-gorica">Nova Gorica</a>, where the Solkan Bridge crosses it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, and enters the Adriatic Sea close to Monfalcone.</p><p>Antiquity recorded the river as Aesontius, Sontius and Isontius. The Slovene name derives from a form borrowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Romance Sontius, which is probably based on an older substrate name meaning a swift, rushing river. Other names are Lusinç in Friulian and Sontig in German.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The present course is the result of several dramatic changes over the past 2,000 years. According to the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, the river called Aesontius, which in Roman times flowed past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> to the Adriatic, was essentially the Natisone and Torre river system. In 585 a landslide cut off the upper part of the Natisone riverbed, and the Bontius River captured the stream. In the following centuries the estuary of this new river, the Soča, moved eastward until it captured the short coastal Sdobba River, through which the Isonzo now reaches the sea.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the river ran parallel to the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. Between May 1915 and November 1917 the valley was the stage of the twelve battles of the Isonzo on the Italian front, in which over half a million Austro-Hungarian and Italian soldiers were killed. The twelfth battle, fought from 24 October to 7 November 1917, is also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caporetto">Battle of Caporetto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is marketed as the Emerald Beauty for its emerald-green water, which is said to keep its colour along its whole length. The Soča Valley attracts visitors to the Big Soča Gorge, the Little Soča Gorge, Kozjak Falls and the Tolmin Gorges. The upper course is home to the marble trout, native to the rivers of the northern Adriatic basin and now endangered. The Italian poet Giuseppe Ungaretti described the river in I fiumi (1916), and the Slovene poet Simon Gregorčič wrote Soči, one of the masterpieces of Slovene poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soča” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eleven costly offensives on the <b>Isonzo</b> were followed by the collapse at Caporetto in 1917 and recovery on the Piave, ending in victory at Vittorio Veneto in 1918; some 600,000 Italian soldiers died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sent by Emperor Zeno to overthrow Odoacer, king of Italy, Theodoric won a battle on the banks of the Isontius River (now the <b>Isonzo</b>), not far from Aquileia, soon after arriving in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They took their name from the castle of Gorizia on the <b>Isonzo</b> river, and in 1253 inherited the County of Tyrol.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-gorizia">County of Gorizia</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeats on the <b>Isonzo</b> and at Verona, Odoacer held out in Ravenna for three years.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-odoacer">Kingdom of Odoacer</a></li></ul></section>
